# Overview
#
# A stale count is a count of a set that the system can grow (`supports
# two modes`). The count goes stale when someone adds a third item, and
# nothing points the editor at the sentence, so the reader gets a wrong
# number. The fix drops the number (`supports these modes`), names each
# item (`supports the single and all modes`), or gives the command that
# lists them (`kubectl api-resources lists the kinds`). The rule sits at
# Recommended, because a count that splits a set in two also matches.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` matches four parts in a row. The first is a verb in `verbs`
# that states the count (`there are`, `supports`, `provides`, `offers`,
# `has`, `ships with`, `includes`, `defines`, `accepts`, or
# `comes with`). The second is a number word in `count`, from `two` to
# `ten`, inclusive. The third is at most one more word. The last is a
# noun in `set` for a set that can grow (`modes`, `backends`, `options`,
# `ways`). Weir cannot read a digit, so `supports 3 modes` passes.
#
# False Positives
#
# `set` leaves out the sets whose nature fixes their size (`ends`,
# `halves`, `parameters`). `the three modes` and `both backends` stay
# out, because in 12 of 14 sampled hits they pointed back at items that
# the same paragraph had listed.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 62 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled
# hits counted a set that can grow. The one misfire split a set in two
# (`two types of system components: those that typically run in a
# container`, and those that do not).
expr verbs [(there are), supports, provides, offers, has, (ships with), includes, defines, accepts, (comes with)]
expr count [two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten]
expr set [
    modes,
    backends,
    providers,
    drivers,
    plugins,
    formats,
    platforms,
    implementations,
    strategies,
    policies,
    profiles,
    levels,
    kinds,
    types,
    options,
    flags,
    subcommands,
    commands,
    endpoints,
    protocols,
    runtimes,
    engines,
    tiers,
    methods,
    algorithms,
    categories,
    versions,
    integrations,
    adapters,
    targets,
    architectures,
    ways
]
expr main (@verbs @count [@set, (* @set)])
let message "Stale count. Drop the number, name each item, or give the command that lists them."
let description "Flags a count of a set that can grow, such as `supports two modes`, which goes stale when the set grows."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The API supports the single and all disruption modes."
allows "The function takes two parameters."
allows "Both backends read the same config."
